<Base title="Urban Mobility" description="Matthew Rossi's engagement with urban mobility, transit advocacy, and civic technology.">

  <div class="page-header">
    <h1>Urban Mobility</h1>
    <p>Advocacy, civic tech, and self-directed work in transit and urban systems.</p>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="civic-tech-heading">
    <h2 id="civic-tech-heading">Civic Technology</h2>
    <p>Living in Chicago put me in contact with the civic-tech community through <a href="https://chihacknight.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ChiHackNight</a>, a weekly gathering where technologists work on open-data projects aimed at urban systems. I built applications using transit system APIs and GTFS feeds — work that surfaced how much policy and institutional decisions shape what data agencies actually make available.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="advocacy-heading">
    <h2 id="advocacy-heading">Advocacy</h2>
    <p>I've been involved in transit and active transportation advocacy through several organizations, including the <a href="https://www.railpassengers.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rail Passengers Association</a>, <a href="https://www.ridersalliance.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Riders Alliance</a> (New York), and <a href="https://www.transalt.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Transportation Alternatives</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="interests-heading">
    <h2 id="interests-heading">Research Interests</h2>
    <p>Transit-oriented development as a tool for aligning land use, housing, and mobility objectives — and the institutional and governance conditions that determine whether it works in practice. Climate adaptation in urban transport systems, and how policy can support resilient, system-wide responses rather than isolated technical fixes.</p>
  </section>

</Base>
